Below is a **complete, self‑contained console program** that shows the most common ways you use C# records.  
It demonstrates

* **Positional records** (shorthand syntax)
* **Non‑positional records** (full property syntax)
* Value‑based equality
* The *with‑expression* (immutable copy with a change)
* Deconstruction
* `init`‑only properties

```csharp
// File: Program.cs
using System;

namespace RecordDemo
{
    /* ----------------------------------------------------
       1. Positional Record
       ---------------------------------------------------- */
    // Declares a record with four positional parameters.
    // C# automatically generates:
    //  • readonly properties (FirstName, LastName, Age)
    //  • constructor that assigns them
    //  • value‑based Equals, GetHashCode, and ToString
    public record Person(string FirstName, string LastName, int Age);

    /* ----------------------------------------------------
       2. Non‑Positional Record (full syntax)
       ---------------------------------------------------- */
    // You can declare a record like a normal class, but
    // properties that are set only with `init` are treated
    // as part of the record’s value.
    public record Address
    {
        public string Street { get; init; }
        public string City   { get; init; }
        public string State  { get; init; }
        public string Zip    { get; init; }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            /* ---------- Positional Record --------- */
            var p1 = new Person("Alice", "Smith", 30);

            // Value‑based equality (structural comparison)
            var p2 = new Person("Alice", "Smith", 30);
            Console.WriteLine($"p1 == p2? {p1 == p2}");   // True

            // With‑expression creates an immutable copy
            var p3 = p1 with { Age = 31 };
            Console.WriteLine($"{p3.FirstName} {p3.LastName} is {p3.Age} years old.");

            // Deconstruction – pulls out the positional fields
            var (first, last, age) = p3;
            Console.WriteLine($"Deconstructed: {first} {last}, {age}");

            /* ---------- Non‑Positional Record ----- */
            var addr = new Address
            {
                Street = "123 Main St",
                City   = "Anytown",
                State  = "CA",
                Zip    = "12345"
            };

            Console.WriteLine($"Address: {addr}");

            // Copying with modification (also uses with‑expression)
            var addr2 = addr with { City = "Othertown" };
            Console.WriteLine($"Updated Address: {addr2}");

            // The init‑only setters can be called only during
            // construction or a with‑expression; trying to set them
            // afterward will result in a compile‑time error:
            // addr.City = "NewCity";   // ❌ Compile‑time error
        }
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see when you run it

```
p1 == p2? True
Alice Smith is 31 years old.
Deconstructed: Alice Smith, 31
Address: Address { Street = 123 Main St, City = Anytown, State = CA, Zip = 12345 }
Updated Address: Address { Street = 123 Main St, City = Othertown, State = CA, Zip = 12345 }
```

---

#### Quick take‑aways

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Record declaration** | `public record Person(string FirstName, string LastName, int Age);` |
| **Value equality** | `==` compares property values, not reference identity. |
| **Immutability** | Properties are `init`‑only; you cannot change them after construction. |
| **With‑expression** | `var p2 = p1 with { Age = 31 };` creates a new record with one changed field. |
| **Deconstruction** | `var (first, last, age) = person;` pulls out the positional fields. |
| **Generated methods** | `Equals`, `GetHashCode`, `ToString` are automatically provided. |

Feel free to paste the code into a new .NET 6/7 console project (`dotnet new console`) and run it to see the records in action.
